pg:CO1 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Convert between epoch timestamps, datetime values and text with time.ctime, fromtimestamp, strftime and strptime, and say which printed results depend on the time zone"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can convert between epoch timestamps, datetime values and text with time.ctime, fromtimestamp, strftime and strptime, and say which printed results depend on the time zone, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter19.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:CO2 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Measure a duration from two clock readings, choose a monotonic clock when the system clock may be set, and pause with time.sleep"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can measure a duration from two clock readings, choose a monotonic clock when the system clock may be set, and pause with time.sleep, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter19.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:CO3 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Compute with dates and durations using datetime and timedelta, for example a date 1000 days ahead, the time between two moments, or the weekday of a date"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can compute with dates and durations using datetime and timedelta, for example a date 1000 days ahead, the time between two moments, or the weekday of a date, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter19.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:CO4 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Distinguish naive from aware datetimes, and explain why utcnow is deprecated and why parsing a month and day without a year warns"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can distinguish naive from aware datetimes, and explain why utcnow is deprecated and why parsing a month and day without a year warns, at the analyze level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter19.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze  .
pg:CO5 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Launch a program with subprocess.run and Popen, read its output and exit code, and choose between a list of arguments and shell=True"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can launch a program with subprocess.run and Popen, read its output and exit code, and choose between a list of arguments and shell=True, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter19.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:CO6 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Choose among the operating system's scheduler, sched and threading.Timer for work that must run later"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can choose among the operating system's scheduler, sched and threading.Timer for work that must run later, at the understand level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter19.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Understand  .
pg:Q1 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A program times a download. Which clock should it read at the start and at the end, and why?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q1_O0, pg:Q1_O1, pg:Q1_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q1_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Understand ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO1 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "time() can be set back by the operating system or by a time service, so the difference of two readings can be negative." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "datetime.now() reads the same adjustable wall clock and, without an argument, has no time zone at all." .
pg:Q1_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "time.time(), because it is the clock of the wall" .
pg:Q1_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "time.monotonic() or time.perf_counter(), because they never go backwards" .
pg:Q1_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "datetime.now(), because it has a time zone" .
pg:Q2 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "What does datetime.fromtimestamp(0, datetime.UTC).isoformat() return?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q2_O0, pg:Q2_O1, pg:Q2_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q2_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO1 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "With a time zone argument the result is aware, so the text ends with the offset +00:00." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The Unix epoch is 1970, not 1900." .
pg:Q2_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'1970-01-01T00:00:00+00:00'" .
pg:Q2_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'1970-01-01T00:00:00'" .
pg:Q2_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'1900-01-01T00:00:00+00:00'" .
pg:Q3 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "How many days are in (datetime(2026, 3, 1) - datetime(2026, 1, 1)).days?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q3_O0, pg:Q3_O1, pg:Q3_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q3_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "January has 31 days and February 2026 has 28, so the difference is 59." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "A timedelta has no months: the subtraction gives days (59), not 2 months." .
pg:Q3_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "60" .
pg:Q3_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "59" .
pg:Q3_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "2" .
pg:Q4 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "datetime.strptime('2026-10-21', '%Y/%m/%d') is run. What happens?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q4_O0, pg:Q4_O1, pg:Q4_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q4_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The slashes of the format do not match the hyphens of the text, so nothing is parsed." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "strptime either returns a datetime or raises; it never returns None." .
pg:Q4_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It returns datetime(2026, 10, 21, 0, 0)" .
pg:Q4_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It raises ValueError, because the text does not match the format" .
pg:Q4_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It returns None" .
pg:Q5 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "What does datetime(2026, 10, 21, 16, 29).strftime('%I:%M %p') return?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q5_O0, pg:Q5_O1, pg:Q5_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q5_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "%I is the hour on a 12-hour clock, so 16 becomes 04." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "%p gives AM or PM in capitals in the C locale, and %I pads the hour to two digits." .
pg:Q5_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'16:29 PM'" .
pg:Q5_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'04:29 PM'" .
pg:Q5_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'4:29 pm'" .
pg:Q6 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "datetime(2026, 1, 1) < datetime(2026, 1, 2, tzinfo=timezone.utc) is evaluated. What happens?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q6_O0, pg:Q6_O1, pg:Q6_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q6_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO3 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The comparison is refused, so no value is returned." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The comparison is refused, so no value is returned." .
pg:Q6_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It returns True" .
pg:Q6_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It raises TypeError, because a naive and an aware datetime cannot be compared" .
pg:Q6_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It returns False" .
pg:Q7 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "With ZoneInfo('America/Los_Angeles'), what is the UTC offset of 12:00 on 1 July 2026 and of 12:00 on 1 January 2026?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q7_O0, pg:Q7_O1, pg:Q7_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q7_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO3 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The zone observes daylight saving time: in July it is PDT, UTC-7." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The offsets are the other way round: the summer time of Los Angeles is UTC-7." .
pg:Q7_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "-07:00 and -08:00" .
pg:Q7_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "-08:00 and -08:00" .
pg:Q7_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "-08:00 and -07:00" .
pg:Q8 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "subprocess.run(['echo', 'hi'], shell=True, capture_output=True, text=True).stdout is what, on Linux?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q8_O0, pg:Q8_O1, pg:Q8_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q8_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO4 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "With shell=True and a list, only the first item is the command and the rest are arguments of the shell itself, so echo prints an empty line." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The command runs; it just does not receive 'hi'." .
pg:Q8_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'hi\\n'" .
pg:Q8_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'\\n'" .
pg:Q8_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "an error is raised" .
pg:Q9 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "What do round(2.5) and round(3.5) return?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q9_O0, pg:Q9_O1, pg:Q9_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q9_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Understand ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO1 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Python rounds a half to the even neighbour, so 2.5 goes down." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "3.5 goes up to 4, the even neighbour." .
pg:Q9_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "3 and 4" .
pg:Q9_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "2 and 4" .
pg:Q9_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "2 and 3" .
pg:Q10 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "str(math.factorial(100000)) raises ValueError on a default Python. Why?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q10_O0, pg:Q10_O1, pg:Q10_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q10_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO1 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Python integers have no upper size; the float limit is not involved." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The call to factorial succeeds; it is the conversion to a string that is limited, and sys.set_int_max_str_digits() changes the limit." .
pg:Q10_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The number is larger than a float" .
pg:Q10_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Converting an integer of more than 4300 digits to text is refused by default" .
pg:Q10_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "factorial() cannot take 100000" .
pg:Q11 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "In a crontab line, what does 0 9 * * 1-5 mean?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q11_O0, pg:Q11_O1, pg:Q11_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q11_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Understand ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO5 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The fifth field restricts the days of the week to 1 to 5, Monday to Friday." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The first field is the minute (0) and the second the hour (9); it is not an interval." .
pg:Q11_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "at 09:00 on every day of the month" .
pg:Q11_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "at 09:00 from Monday to Friday" .
pg:Q11_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "every 9 minutes on weekdays" .
pg:EpochClock a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Epoch clock"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter opens with the system clock: the time module reads it, time.time() returns the number of seconds since the Unix epoch, time.sleep() pauses a program, and Project 14, the Super Stopwatch, subtracts two readings to time laps (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation defines the epoch as the point where the time begins, 1 January 1970 00:00:00 UTC on all platforms it describes, and lists clocks that differ in whether they can go backwards (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Stinner, 2012)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_EpochClock> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-EpochClock" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:EpochClock .
pg:EpochTimestamp a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Epoch timestamp"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says time.time() returns a float of the seconds that have passed since the Unix epoch, which it calls the reference moment of 1 January 1970 UTC, and calls the value an epoch timestamp (Sweigart, 2025). POSIX defines the seconds since the epoch by an expression of the year, day of the year, hour, minute and second in which a day has exactly 86400 seconds (IEEE and The Open Group, 2024)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_EpochTimestamp> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-EpochTimestamp" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:EpochClock .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:EpochTimestamp .
pg:EpochZero a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Epoch zero"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter defines the Unix epoch as January 1, 1970, at midnight, UTC, a standard reference time for many programming languages, and says time.time() returns the epoch timestamp as a float of the seconds since then (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation says the epoch is the point where the time starts and that gmtime(0) shows it (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_EpochZero> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-EpochZero" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:EpochTimestamp .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:EpochZero .
pg:PosixFormula a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Posix formula"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter does not say how a moment becomes a count of seconds, and POSIX gives the expression: tm_sec + tm_min*60 + tm_hour*3600 + tm_yday*86400 plus a term per year, with every day exactly 86400 seconds long (IEEE and The Open Group, 2024). The Python documentation adds that leap seconds are not counted in the epoch timestamp (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_PosixFormula> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PosixFormula" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:EpochTimestamp .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PosixFormula .
pg:FloatPrecision a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Float precision"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter prints time.time() as 1773813875.3518236 and later calls round() on differences because the values have many digits after the decimal point (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation says the return value is a float and that not all systems provide time with a better precision than 1 second (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_FloatPrecision> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FloatPrecision" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:EpochTimestamp .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FloatPrecision .
pg:ClockKinds a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Clock kinds"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter profiles a loop by subtracting one time.time() reading from another, and the Python documentation lists several clocks that differ in whether they are monotonic and whether they can be adjusted (Sweigart, 2025)(Python Software Foundation, 2026). PEP 418 added monotonic() and perf_counter() because the system clock can go backwards (Stinner, 2012)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_ClockKinds> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ClockKinds" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:EpochClock .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ClockKinds .
pg:ClockInfoTable a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Clock info table"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter uses only time.time() to measure time, and the Python documentation says time.monotonic() cannot go backwards and is not affected by system clock updates, while perf_counter() has the highest available resolution for short durations (Sweigart, 2025)(Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Stinner, 2012). get_clock_info() reports the implementation and whether a clock is monotonic or adjustable (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_ClockInfoTable> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ClockInfoTable" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ClockKinds .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ClockInfoTable .
pg:SteppedWallClock a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Stepped wall clock"@en ;
    skos:definition "The stopwatch subtracts one time.time() reading from another, which the chapter treats as elapsed time (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation says time.time() may return a lower value than a previous call if the system clock has been set back between the two calls, which a monotonic clock cannot do (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Stinner, 2012)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_SteppedWallClock> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SteppedWallClock" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ClockKinds .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SteppedWallClock .
pg:RoundFunction a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Round function"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter uses round() on both times of the stopwatch and Practice Question 5 asks what it returns; Appendix B answers that it returns the closest integer to its argument (Sweigart, 2025)(Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation says that with no digits round() returns an integer and that values are rounded to the closest multiple of 10 to the power minus ndigits, with ties going to the even choice (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_RoundFunction> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RoundFunction" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ClockKinds .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RoundFunction .
pg:ProfilingByClock a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Profiling by clock"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter times calcProd() with time.time() and prints that it took 2.844162940979004 seconds, and the method follows Chapter 13 of the author's other book (Sweigart, 2025)(Sweigart, 2020). The Python documentation of timeit says to use perf_counter for such timings and the int to string conversion limit of 4300 digits applies to the product (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_ProfilingByClock> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ProfilingByClock" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ClockKinds .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ProfilingByClock .
pg:PausingPrograms a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Pausing programs"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says time.sleep() blocks, that is it will not return, until the number of seconds passed has elapsed, and uses it to count seconds in the stopwatch and to wait in a loop until Halloween 2039 (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation says the sleep is at least the time asked unless a signal handler raises an exception, a rule that PEP 475 introduced in Python 3.5 (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Natali and Stinner, 2014)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_PausingPrograms> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PausingPrograms" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:EpochClock .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PausingPrograms .
pg:SleepFunction a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Sleep function"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says time.sleep() will block until the number of seconds you passed has elapsed, and Practice Question 4 answers time.sleep(5) (Sweigart, 2025)(Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation says the suspension time may be longer than requested by an arbitrary amount because of the scheduling of other activity (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_SleepFunction> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SleepFunction" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PausingPrograms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SleepFunction .
pg:SleepAndSignals a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Sleep and signals"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter suggests that a program can print a message such as Press CTRL-C to cancel and handle KeyboardInterrupt around a sleep (Sweigart, 2025). Since Python 3.5 a sleep is retried after a signal handler returns, and a handler that raises an exception ends it, a rule PEP 475 introduced (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Natali and Stinner, 2014)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_SleepAndSignals> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SleepAndSignals" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PausingPrograms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SleepAndSignals .
pg:PollingLoops a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Polling loops"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's pause until Halloween 2039 is a while loop that calls time.sleep(1) between checks so that the computer does not waste CPU processing cycles by checking the time over and over as fast as possible (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation of the time module counts CPU time with process_time (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_PollingLoops> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PollingLoops" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PausingPrograms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PollingLoops .
pg:SleepDrift a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Sleep drift"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's loop sleeps one second per round, and each round also takes the time of its own work (Sweigart, 2025). A loop that sleeps a fixed interval after the work accumulates the work as drift, which a loop that sleeps until the next tick of a monotonic clock avoids (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_SleepDrift> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SleepDrift" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PausingPrograms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SleepDrift .
pg:DatetimeValues a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Datetime values"@en ;
    skos:definition "The second half of the time material is the datetime module: datetime objects hold a moment, timedelta objects hold a duration, strftime() turns a moment into text with the directives of Table 19-1, and strptime() reads text back into a moment (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation describes the types as naive or aware and lists the directives and the rules for two-digit years (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_DatetimeValues> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DatetimeValues" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DatetimeValues .
pg:MomentsAndFields a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Moments and fields"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter makes a datetime with datetime.datetime(2026, 10, 21, 16, 29, 0), reads the year, month, day, hour, minute and second attributes, calls datetime.datetime.now(), and compares moments with the usual operators (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation lists the same fields and adds microsecond, tzinfo and fold (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_MomentsAndFields> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MomentsAndFields" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DatetimeValues .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MomentsAndFields .
pg:DatetimeObject a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Datetime object"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter makes a moment with datetime.datetime(2026, 10, 21, 16, 29, 0) and says the datetime function takes year, month, day, hour, minute and second, with hour, minute and second defaulting to 0 (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation says the year, month and day arguments are required and that out-of-range values raise ValueError (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_DatetimeObject> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DatetimeObject" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:MomentsAndFields .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DatetimeObject .
pg:CurrentMoment a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Current moment"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls datetime.datetime.now() for the current moment and datetime.datetime.fromtimestamp(time.time()) for the same moment from an epoch timestamp (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation says now() returns the current local date and time and, with a tz argument, the time in that zone (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_CurrentMoment> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CurrentMoment" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:MomentsAndFields .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CurrentMoment .
pg:DatetimeComparison a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Datetime comparison"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter compares halloween_2026 with oct_31_2026 and new_years_2027 with the operators ==, > and says a datetime later in time is greater (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation says naive and aware values cannot be compared for order and that equality of naive and aware values is always false (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_DatetimeComparison> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DatetimeComparison" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:MomentsAndFields .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DatetimeComparison .
pg:WeekdayOfDate a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Weekday of date"@en ;
    skos:definition "Practice Question 7 asks which day of the week 7 January 2019 was, and Appendix B answers with weekday() equal to 0, Monday (Sweigart, 2025)(Sweigart, 2025). The documentation says weekday() returns Monday as 0 and isoweekday() returns Monday as 1 (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_WeekdayOfDate> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WeekdayOfDate" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:MomentsAndFields .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WeekdayOfDate .
pg:DurationsTimedelta a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Durations timedelta"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter makes a timedelta with keyword arguments for weeks, days, hours, minutes, seconds and the smaller units, reads its days, seconds and microseconds attributes, uses total_seconds(), adds 1000 days to now and subtracts about thirty years from 21 October 2026 (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation says all arguments are optional and default to 0 and that only days, seconds and microseconds are stored (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_DurationsTimedelta> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DurationsTimedelta" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DatetimeValues .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DurationsTimedelta .
pg:TimedeltaObject a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Timedelta object"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter makes a timedelta of 11 days, 10 hours, 9 minutes and 8 seconds and shows its days, seconds and microseconds attributes as (11, 36548, 0), and says total_seconds() returns the duration in seconds (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation says only days, seconds and microseconds are stored and are normalised, so the seconds are in the range 0 to 86399 (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_TimedeltaObject> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TimedeltaObject" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DurationsTimedelta .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TimedeltaObject .
pg:NoMonthsOrYears a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "No months or years"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says there is no month or year keyword argument because a month or year is a variable amount of time depending on the particular month or year (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation lists the seven arguments and no larger unit (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_NoMonthsOrYears> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-NoMonthsOrYears" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DurationsTimedelta .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:NoMonthsOrYears .
pg:DurationArithmetic a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Duration arithmetic"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter adds thousand_days, a timedelta of 1000 days, to now and gets 28 August 2029, and says timedelta objects can be added to or subtracted from datetime objects (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation lists the operations with another timedelta, an int or float, and a datetime (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_DurationArithmetic> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DurationArithmetic" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DurationsTimedelta .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DurationArithmetic .
pg:ThirtyYearsError a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Thirty years error"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter subtracts about_thirty_years, timedelta(days=365 * 30), from 21 October 2026 and says it ignores leap years, and it prints 28 October 1996 (Sweigart, 2025). The text before the example calls the module function datetime.delta(), which does not exist (Sweigart, 2025)(Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_ThirtyYearsError> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ThirtyYearsError" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DurationsTimedelta .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ThirtyYearsError .
pg:FormatAndParse a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Format and parse"@en ;
    skos:definition "Table 19-1 of the chapter lists strftime directives from %Y to %%, strftime() turns a datetime into text and strptime() reads text into a datetime with the same directives, and the chapter sends the reader to strftime.org for the full list (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation has two tables of the directives, one for the C standard and one for Python, with notes on platform differences (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_FormatAndParse> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FormatAndParse" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DatetimeValues .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FormatAndParse .
pg:DirectiveTable a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Directive table"@en ;
    skos:definition "Table 19-1 lists the strftime directives %Y, %y, %m, %B, %b, %d, %j, %w, %A, %a, %H, %I, %M, %S, %p and %%, with a column for the meaning (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation lists more directives and says some depend on the platform and the locale (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_DirectiveTable> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DirectiveTable" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FormatAndParse .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DirectiveTable .
pg:StrftimeFormat a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Strftime format"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls oct_21st.strftime('%Y/%m/%d %H:%M:%S') and gets 2026/10/21 16:29:00, and also formats %I:%M %p and a custom text containing the directives (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation says directives other than those of the C standard may differ between platforms (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_StrftimeFormat> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-StrftimeFormat" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FormatAndParse .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:StrftimeFormat .
pg:StrptimeParse a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Strptime parse"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls datetime.datetime.strptime('October 21, 2026', '%B %d, %Y') and says the string must match the format exactly or it raises ValueError (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation says strptime is a class method that parses a string by a format, and 3.14 added date.strptime and time.strptime (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_StrptimeParse> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-StrptimeParse" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FormatAndParse .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:StrptimeParse .
pg:TwoDigitYear a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Two digit year"@en ;
    skos:definition "Table 19-1 says %y is the year without century, '00' to '99' (1970 to 2069) (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation says values 69 to 99 are mapped to 1969 to 1999 and values 0 to 68 to 2000 to 2068, following POSIX and ISO C (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(IEEE and The Open Group, 2024)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_TwoDigitYear> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TwoDigitYear" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FormatAndParse .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TwoDigitYear .
pg:YearlessParsing a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Yearless parsing"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's strptime examples all include a year (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation says that parsing a day and a month without a year always uses 1900, which is not a leap year, so 29 February fails, and that the behaviour will change (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_YearlessParsing> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-YearlessParsing" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FormatAndParse .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:YearlessParsing .
pg:IsoFormats a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Iso formats"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter writes dates as 2026/10/21 and October 21, 2026 and does not mention the international standard (Sweigart, 2025). ISO 8601 and RFC 3339 write 2026-10-21T16:29:07+00:00, and the documentation says fromisoformat accepts most ISO 8601 formats since 3.11 (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Klyne and Newman, 2002)(Wikipedia, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_IsoFormats> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-IsoFormats" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FormatAndParse .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:IsoFormats .
pg:TimeZonesAndPitfalls a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Time zones and pitfalls"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter never uses a time zone object, yet it prints times of day and says that its examples were run in Pacific Standard Time (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation says that a naive datetime does not contain time zone information and that the zoneinfo module provides the IANA time zone database (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Ganssle, 2020)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_TimeZonesAndPitfalls> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TimeZonesAndPitfalls" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TimeZonesAndPitfalls .
pg:ZoneAwareness a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Zone awareness"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's outputs depend on the zone of the author's computer, which it names once as Pacific Standard Time (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation separates naive values from aware values and says that ZoneInfo gives an IANA time zone with the daylight saving time rules (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Internet Assigned Numbers Authority, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_ZoneAwareness> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ZoneAwareness" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TimeZonesAndPitfalls .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ZoneAwareness .
pg:NaiveVersusAware a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Naive versus aware"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's datetime values carry no zone, and the book does not use the words naive or aware (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation says an aware object can locate itself relative to other aware objects, a naive object cannot, and that comparing the two for order raises TypeError (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_NaiveVersusAware> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-NaiveVersusAware" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ZoneAwareness .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:NaiveVersusAware .
pg:UtcnowDeprecated a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Utcnow deprecated"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter uses datetime.datetime.now() and does not use utcnow(), which many older programs use for UTC (Sweigart, 2025). Python 3.12 deprecated utcnow() and utcfromtimestamp() because they return naive values, and the documentation names now(UTC) and fromtimestamp(ts, UTC) as the replacements (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_UtcnowDeprecated> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-UtcnowDeprecated" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ZoneAwareness .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:UtcnowDeprecated .
pg:ZoneInfoZones a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Zone info zones"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says nothing about zones beyond the author's own, and the zoneinfo module appeared in Python 3.9 with the data taken from the system or the tzdata package (Sweigart, 2025)(Ganssle, 2020). The IANA database is the source of the zone rules and names (Internet Assigned Numbers Authority, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_ZoneInfoZones> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ZoneInfoZones" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ZoneAwareness .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ZoneInfoZones .
pg:FromTimestampZones a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "From timestamp zones"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls datetime.datetime.fromtimestamp(1000000) and prints datetime.datetime(1970, 1, 12, 5, 46, 40), a reading that is the local clock of the author's zone (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation says fromtimestamp without a tz gives the local date and time of the platform (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_FromTimestampZones> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FromTimestampZones" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ZoneAwareness .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FromTimestampZones .
pg:PstVersusPdt a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Pst versus pdt"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says it calls time.time() on March 17, 2026, at 11:04 PM Pacific Standard Time (Sweigart, 2025). The United States was on daylight saving time from 8 March 2026, and the IANA data give the abbreviation PDT and the offset -07:00 for Los Angeles on that day (Internet Assigned Numbers Authority, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_PstVersusPdt> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PstVersusPdt" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ZoneAwareness .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PstVersusPdt .
pg:DaylightSaving a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Daylight saving"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter adds timedelta values to datetime values and does not say that a day is not always 24 hours (Sweigart, 2025). PEP 495 added the fold attribute so that a time that happens twice on the day the clocks go back can be told apart (Belopolsky and Peters, 2015)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_DaylightSaving> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DaylightSaving" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TimeZonesAndPitfalls .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DaylightSaving .
pg:WallClockArithmetic a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Wall clock arithmetic"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's thousand-day and thirty-year examples use naive values, for which a day is 24 hours by definition (Sweigart, 2025). For an aware value the documentation says arithmetic acts on the wall clock and ignores the change of offset, so it recommends converting to UTC to add real durations (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_WallClockArithmetic> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WallClockArithmetic" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DaylightSaving .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WallClockArithmetic .
pg:GapAndFold a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Gap and fold"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter does not meet local times that do not exist or exist twice, and PEP 495 added fold so that the second pass through an hour can be told from the first (Sweigart, 2025)(Belopolsky and Peters, 2015). The documentation says a time in the gap is invalid and that ambiguous times take fold=0 by default (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_GapAndFold> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-GapAndFold" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DaylightSaving .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:GapAndFold .
pg:LimitsOfTime a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Limits of time"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter counts time from 1970 and adds thousands of days, and it does not say where the counting ends (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation says the year of a date is limited to 1 through 9999 and that the date type uses the Gregorian calendar in both directions (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_LimitsOfTime> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LimitsOfTime" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TimeZonesAndPitfalls .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LimitsOfTime .
pg:Year2038 a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Year2038"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter counts seconds with a float and does not mention that C programs have counted them in 32 bits (Sweigart, 2025). A signed 32-bit time_t overflows at 03:14:07 UTC on 19 January 2038, and Python on a 64-bit platform has no such limit (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Wikipedia, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_Year2038> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Year2038" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LimitsOfTime .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Year2038 .
pg:DatetimeRange a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Datetime range"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's dates lie between 1970 and 2039, far from the limits of the type (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation gives MINYEAR as 1 and MAXYEAR as 9999 and says date arithmetic past the ends raises OverflowError (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_DatetimeRange> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DatetimeRange" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LimitsOfTime .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DatetimeRange .
pg:GregorianCalendar a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Gregorian calendar"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter uses dates from 1970 to 2039 and the Gregorian rules without naming them (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation calls date an idealized naive date, assuming the current Gregorian calendar always was, and always will be, in effect (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Wikipedia, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_GregorianCalendar> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-GregorianCalendar" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LimitsOfTime .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:GregorianCalendar .
pg:LaunchingPrograms a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Launching programs"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's third part launches other programs from Python: subprocess.run() starts a program and waits, subprocess.Popen() starts it and returns at once, a list of strings carries the command line arguments, capture_output=True collects the text, and start or open opens a file with its default application (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation says that the recommended approach for invoking subprocesses is the run() function for all use cases it can handle (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_LaunchingPrograms> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LaunchingPrograms" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LaunchingPrograms .
pg:RunAndPopen a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Run and popen"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says subprocess.run() launches a process, waits for it and returns a CompletedProcess, and that Popen returns a Popen object at once whose poll() returns None while the process runs and whose wait() blocks (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation describes run() as the recommended approach and Popen as the underlying interface (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_RunAndPopen> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RunAndPopen" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LaunchingPrograms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RunAndPopen .
pg:RunWaits a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Run waits"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls subprocess.run(['C:\\\\Windows\\\\System32\\\\calc.exe']) and shows CompletedProcess(args=['C:\\\\Windows\\\\System32\\\\calc.exe'], returncode=0), and says run() waits (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation says run() waits for the command to complete and returns a CompletedProcess instance (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_RunWaits> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RunWaits" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RunAndPopen .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RunWaits .
pg:PopenNoWait a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Popen no wait"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says Popen returns a Popen object at once, that poll() returns None while the process runs and an exit code afterwards, and that wait() blocks until the process ends (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation adds that wait(timeout) raises TimeoutExpired (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_PopenNoWait> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PopenNoWait" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RunAndPopen .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PopenNoWait .
pg:CheckAndTimeout a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Check and timeout"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's runs all return 0 and it does not discuss failure of the launched program (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation says check=True raises CalledProcessError for a non-zero code and that timeout kills the child and raises TimeoutExpired (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_CheckAndTimeout> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CheckAndTimeout" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RunAndPopen .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CheckAndTimeout .
pg:ArgumentsAndOutput a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Arguments and output"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says to pass a list as the sole argument to run(), that the first string is the executable file name, that the other strings are the command line arguments and that the list is in effect the sys.argv of the launched program; it collects the output of ping with capture_output=True and text=True (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation warns that shell=True can be a security hazard if the command includes unsanitized input and points to shlex.quote (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_ArgumentsAndOutput> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ArgumentsAndOutput" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LaunchingPrograms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ArgumentsAndOutput .
pg:ArgumentList a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Argument list"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says the first string of the list is the executable and the others are the arguments, and that the list is in effect the sys.argv of the launched program (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation says that on POSIX with shell=False the list is the argument vector, so no splitting or expansion happens (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_ArgumentList> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ArgumentList" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ArgumentsAndOutput .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ArgumentList .
pg:CaptureOutput a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Capture output"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter runs subprocess.run(['ping', '-n', '4', 'nostarch.com'], capture_output=True, text=True) and prints the Windows output of ping (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation says capture_output is shorthand for stdout=PIPE and stderr=PIPE, that text=True gives str, and that it cannot be used with stdout (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_CaptureOutput> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CaptureOutput" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ArgumentsAndOutput .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CaptureOutput .
pg:ShellTrueList a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Shell true list"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter runs subprocess.run(['start', 'hello.txt'], shell=True) and explains that the operating system knows the file associations (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation says that on POSIX with shell=True and a sequence, the first item is the command string and any additional items are arguments to the shell itself (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_ShellTrueList> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ShellTrueList" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ArgumentsAndOutput .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ShellTrueList .
pg:ShellInjection a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Shell injection"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter builds no command from user input, and the documentation says shell=True can be a security hazard if the command includes unsanitized input from an untrusted source (Sweigart, 2025)(Python Software Foundation, 2026). shlex.quote returns a shell-escaped version of a string (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_ShellInjection> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ShellInjection" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ArgumentsAndOutput .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ShellInjection .
pg:ExitAndStopping a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Exit and stopping"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says poll() returns None while a process runs and its exit code when it ends, and shows the code 0 (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation says a negative returncode -N means the child was terminated by signal N on POSIX (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_ExitAndStopping> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ExitAndStopping" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LaunchingPrograms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ExitAndStopping .
pg:ExitCodes a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Exit codes"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter shows returncode=0 for each run and says poll() returns the exit code of a terminated process (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation says a negative returncode -N means the child was terminated by signal N on POSIX (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_ExitCodes> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ExitCodes" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ExitAndStopping .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ExitCodes .
pg:TerminateVsKill a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Terminate vs kill"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter does not stop a launched process, and the documentation says terminate() sends SIGTERM and kill() sends SIGKILL on POSIX (Sweigart, 2025)(Python Software Foundation, 2026). The signal documentation says SIGKILL cannot be caught or ignored (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_TerminateVsKill> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TerminateVsKill" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ExitAndStopping .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TerminateVsKill .
pg:OpeningFilesWithDefaultApps a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Opening files with default apps"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter opens a text file with start on Windows and open on macOS and Linux, through subprocess.run() with shell=True for start, and names pyperclip in its practice program and playsound3 for the sound (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation of webbrowser adds the module for URLs and that of pyperclip lists the programs it needs on Linux (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Sweigart, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_OpeningFilesWithDefaultApps> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-OpeningFilesWithDefaultApps" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LaunchingPrograms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:OpeningFilesWithDefaultApps .
pg:OpenCommand a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Open command"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that on Windows the equivalent of double-clicking is the start command and that on macOS and Linux this is the open command, and its countdown comments pass 'open' for macOS and Linux (Sweigart, 2025). The freedesktop project's tool for Linux is xdg-open, which opens a file in the user's preferred application (Freedesktop.org, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_OpenCommand> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-OpenCommand" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:OpeningFilesWithDefaultApps .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:OpenCommand .
pg:Webbrowser a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Webbrowser"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter suggests webbrowser.open() to open a website at the end of the countdown (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation says open returns True if a browser was successfully launched and False otherwise (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_Webbrowser> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Webbrowser" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:OpeningFilesWithDefaultApps .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Webbrowser .
pg:ClipboardAndSound a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Clipboard and sound"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Practice Program copies its output with pyperclip, which the chapter introduced in Chapter 8, and the chapter names playsound3 from Chapter 12 for sound files (Sweigart, 2025). The Appendix A of the book pins playsound3==2.4.0, and PyPI shows 3.3.2 as the newest release (Sweigart, 2025)(Python Package Index, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_ClipboardAndSound> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ClipboardAndSound" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:OpeningFilesWithDefaultApps .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ClipboardAndSound .
pg:SchedulingTasks a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Scheduling tasks"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter hands scheduling to the operating system in one paragraph: Task Scheduler on Windows, launchd on macOS and cron on Linux, with instructions it says are at the book's page at No Starch Press (Sweigart, 2025). The Python standard library offers only schedulers that live inside a running process: the sched module, threading.Timer and the call_later method of an asyncio event loop (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_SchedulingTasks> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SchedulingTasks" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SchedulingTasks .
pg:OsSchedulers a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Os schedulers"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Summary of the chapter says the operating system's scheduler saves writing clock-checking code, and the section Running Task Scheduler, launchd, and cron names the three programs (Sweigart, 2025). A crontab line has five time fields and a command, and when both day fields are restricted the command runs when either matches (IEEE and The Open Group, 2024)(Vixie and Debian, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_OsSchedulers> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-OsSchedulers" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SchedulingTasks .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:OsSchedulers .
pg:CronFields a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Cron fields"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter names cron as the Linux scheduler and gives no syntax (Sweigart, 2025). A crontab line has the minute, hour, day of month, month and day of week fields and the command, and when both day fields are restricted the command runs when either matches the current time (IEEE and The Open Group, 2024)(Vixie and Debian, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_CronFields> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CronFields" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:OsSchedulers .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CronFields .
pg:CronEnvironment a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Cron environment"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter does not say what a job started by cron sees, and the Debian manual page says cron sets up some environment variables itself and runs commands with /bin/sh (Sweigart, 2025)(Vixie and Debian, 2026). The manual page of the cron version Debian ships does not mention CRON_TZ (Vixie and Debian, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_CronEnvironment> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CronEnvironment" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:OsSchedulers .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CronEnvironment .
pg:LaunchdPlist a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Launchd plist"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter names launchd and no file format, and launchd reads jobs from property lists with a Label, ProgramArguments and StartCalendarInterval (Sweigart, 2025)(Apple, 2026). The plistlib module reads and writes such files (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_LaunchdPlist> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LaunchdPlist" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:OsSchedulers .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LaunchdPlist .
pg:SystemdCalendar a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Systemd calendar"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter does not mention systemd, which most Linux distributions use for timers; a timer unit has an OnCalendar expression such as Mon..Fri 09:00 (Sweigart, 2025)(Freedesktop.org, 2026). systemd-analyze calendar normalises an expression and prints the next time it fires (Freedesktop.org, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_SystemdCalendar> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SystemdCalendar" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:OsSchedulers .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SystemdCalendar .
pg:InProcessScheduling a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "In process scheduling"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's own way to wait for a date is a while loop that calls time.sleep(1) until datetime.now() reaches Halloween 2039 (Sweigart, 2025). The standard library has the sched module with a priority queue of events, threading.Timer for one call after a delay and the asyncio loop methods call_later and call_at (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_InProcessScheduling> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-InProcessScheduling" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SchedulingTasks .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:InProcessScheduling .
pg:SchedModule a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Sched module"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's alternative to the operating system scheduler is its own loop with time.sleep(1) (Sweigart, 2025). The sched module keeps events in a queue ordered by time then priority, and enter and enterabs add them, cancel removes one and run executes them (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_SchedModule> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SchedModule" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:InProcessScheduling .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SchedModule .
pg:TimerThread a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Timer thread"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter does not use threads, and threading.Timer is a Thread that calls a function once after an interval, with cancel() to stop it before it fires (Sweigart, 2025)(Python Software Foundation, 2026). A Timer is a subclass of Thread and is not a daemon unless set (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_TimerThread> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TimerThread" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:InProcessScheduling .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TimerThread .
pg:AsyncioCallLater a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Asyncio call later"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter does not use asyncio, and the event loop methods call_later(delay, callback) and call_at(when, callback) schedule a callback (Sweigart, 2025)(Python Software Foundation, 2026). call_soon schedules it for the next iteration of the loop (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_AsyncioCallLater> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AsyncioCallLater" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:InProcessScheduling .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AsyncioCallLater .
pg:ScheduleLibrary a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Schedule library"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter names no third-party scheduler, and the schedule package, version 1.2.2 on PyPI from 2024, runs jobs written as every().day.at('10:30').do(job) while a program keeps calling run_pending (Sweigart, 2025)(Python Package Index, 2026). It is not an operating system scheduler and stops with its process (Python Package Index, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_ScheduleLibrary> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ScheduleLibrary" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:InProcessScheduling .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ScheduleLibrary .
pg:BookAndPractice a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Book and practice"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter ends with Project 14, the Super Stopwatch, Project 15, the Simple Countdown, a summary, seven practice questions with their answers in Appendix B and two practice programs, the Prettified Stopwatch and the Friday the 13th finder (Sweigart, 2025)(Sweigart, 2025). The online materials ship three files for the chapter (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_BookAndPractice> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BookAndPractice" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BookAndPractice .
pg:ListingsAsPrinted a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Listings as printed"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter prints 29 listings, 20 of them interactive sessions, and the online materials ship three files for it (Sweigart, 2025). The book's Windows listings, such as subprocess.run(['C:\\\\Windows\\\\notepad.exe', 'C:\\\\Users\\Al\\\\hello.txt']), are part of those listings (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_ListingsAsPrinted> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ListingsAsPrinted" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BookAndPractice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ListingsAsPrinted .
pg:EnDashListings a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "En dash listings"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's listings write end_time – start_time and time.time() – last_time and oct_21st – about_thirty_years, and the text says to subtract (Sweigart, 2025). The Python grammar has no use for the en dash U+2013, which the compiler reports as an invalid character (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_EnDashListings> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-EnDashListings" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ListingsAsPrinted .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:EnDashListings .
pg:ShippedFiles a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Shipped files"@en ;
    skos:definition "The online materials of the 3rd edition hold calcProd.py, stopwatch.py and simplecountdown.py for this chapter and no alarm.wav, hello.txt, prettifiedStopwatch.py or fridayThirteenth.py, and no document about the operating system schedulers (Sweigart, 2025)(No Starch Press, 2025). The download is a zip of 91 files (No Starch Press, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_ShippedFiles> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ShippedFiles" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ListingsAsPrinted .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ShippedFiles .
pg:ReplayOfListings a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Replay of listings"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter shows 20 interactive sessions, which a reader types into the shell and compares with the printed text (Sweigart, 2025). A comparison of a book's outputs with a new run separates changes of Python from the clock and the platform of the author (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_ReplayOfListings> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ReplayOfListings" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ListingsAsPrinted .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ReplayOfListings .
pg:SuperStopwatch a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Super stopwatch"@en ;
    skos:definition "Project 14 asks for a stopwatch that prints the lap number, the total time and the lap time each time the user presses ENTER, with the instructions printed first and round(..., 2) on both times (Sweigart, 2025). The practice program rewrites the output in columns with rjust and ljust and copies it to the clipboard with pyperclip (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_SuperStopwatch> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SuperStopwatch" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BookAndPractice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SuperStopwatch .
pg:LapArithmetic a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Lap arithmetic"@en ;
    skos:definition "Project 14 computes lap_time as round(time.time() - last_time, 2) and total_time as round(time.time() - start_time, 2) and prints Lap #1: 3.56(3.56) (Sweigart, 2025). The printed example in the Practice Program shows the same numbers in columns (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_LapArithmetic> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LapArithmetic" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SuperStopwatch .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LapArithmetic .
pg:ColumnFormatting a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Column formatting"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Prettified Stopwatch asks for the lap number, total time and lap time lined up in columns with rjust() and ljust(), with the output Lap # 1:   3.56 (  3.56) (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation of str says rjust pads on the left to a width and ljust on the right (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_ColumnFormatting> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ColumnFormatting" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SuperStopwatch .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ColumnFormatting .
pg:SimpleCountdown a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Simple countdown"@en ;
    skos:definition "Project 15 counts down from 60, prints the time left, sleeps a second per number and then plays alarm.wav by opening it with start or open, with the Windows line commented out in the listing (Sweigart, 2025). The sound file is at autbor.com (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_SimpleCountdown> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SimpleCountdown" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BookAndPractice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SimpleCountdown .
pg:CountdownLoop a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Countdown loop"@en ;
    skos:definition "Project 15 sets time_left = 60, prints it, sleeps one second and subtracts one in a while loop that runs while time_left is greater than 0 (Sweigart, 2025). The loop sleeps a fixed second after the print and so ends a little later than 60 seconds (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_CountdownLoop> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CountdownLoop" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SimpleCountdown .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CountdownLoop .
pg:AlarmSound a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Alarm sound"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says to play the sound file by opening alarm.wav with the default application, and gives autbor.com/alarm.wav as the place of the file (Sweigart, 2025). The short link redirects to the sound file at automatetheboringstuff.com (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_AlarmSound> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AlarmSound" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SimpleCountdown .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AlarmSound .
pg:PracticePrograms a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Practice programs"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter asks for a Friday the 13th finder in two forms: one that walks forward from the current date and one that works out when the next Friday the 13th falls, and answers seven questions in Appendix B (Sweigart, 2025)(Sweigart, 2025). The weekday() method returns 0 for Monday and the weekday for 13 is 4 for Friday (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_PracticePrograms> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PracticePrograms" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BookAndPractice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PracticePrograms .
pg:FridayForward a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Friday forward"@en ;
    skos:definition "The first practice program asks for a program that finds the next Friday the 13th, and the chapter hints at weekday() and a loop that adds a timedelta of one day (Sweigart, 2025). weekday() returns 4 for Friday and the day of the month 13 is checked with .day (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_FridayForward> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FridayForward" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticePrograms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FridayForward .
pg:FridayBackward a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Friday backward"@en ;
    skos:definition "The second practice program asks for the program run in the other direction, going back one day at a time (Sweigart, 2025). The range of the date type ends at the year 1, where subtracting a day raises OverflowError (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_FridayBackward> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FridayBackward" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticePrograms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FridayBackward .
pg:AppendixBAnswers a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Appendix b answers"@en ;
    skos:definition "Appendix B answers Question 3 with time.asctime(), Question 5 with the closest integer and Question 7 with weekday() equal to 0 (Sweigart, 2025)(Sweigart, 2025). The documentation says asctime() formats like ctime() but takes a time tuple, and with no argument uses localtime() (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch19#S_AppendixBAnswers> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AppendixBAnswers" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticePrograms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AppendixBAnswers .
